                                   BEFORE

Before any of this there was a river, and a woman who was done
counting.
    Amaia had started the night with fourteen. By the third night on the
water there were eleven: two lost to a passage that looked open by day‐
light and shut like a fist after dark, one to fever. She had stopped saying
their names. A name was a door, and the door opened downward.
    Her brother Dairon took the lead canoe through the mangroves with
the silence of a man who had spent all his fear and was running on
something meaner. Yara rode behind him with the wrapped cemí
against her chest, the cloth soaked and dried and soaked again. Beheto,
whose lungs rattled when he breathed hard and whose timing had
never once been wrong, sat in the second canoe with his drum between
his knees. In the third, three children sat so still it was worse than cry‐
ing. The behique called them the lit ones. He meant it plainly. Amaia
had seen blue come off their skin in the dark, in fever, in the second
after lightning, and be gone before she could swear to it. Her own son,
not yet two, slept against her ribs.
    Behind them, on the bank, the men who had trailed them for two
months had stopped. One carried a small brass bell on a pole and rang
it, and the sound it made was flat and wrong and crawled up her spine.



