                        HERALDS OF THE CEMÍ



The three children flinched as one, and where they flinched a thin blue
came off their skin and leaned toward the bank, toward the bell, like
smoke toward an open door. Another man sat on a stump with a book
across his knee and wrote in it as the canoes slid away, quick and neat,
the way you count sacks.
   That was what she would remember. The pen, moving.
   “There’s a path under the roots,” said the seven-year-old, to the wa‐
ter and not to them. Nobody told her to hush. They had all learned
what it cost to dismiss the lit ones when they spoke like that. Dairon
turned the bow. The passage was so narrow the wood screamed against
the roots, and then it opened, and the air smelled of salt from the
wrong direction.
   “They found where we were,” the nine-year-old said, listening to the
bell. “Not where we are.”
   Beheto laid his palms on the drumhead. What he played did not
boom outward. It dropped, down through the hull and into the water
and the roots and the held breath of the earth under both, and it said
the one thing worth saying to a river: we are not taking, we are asking.
The current changed. No walls of water, no hand from the sky. The ca‐
noes simply moved as if carried, by something that owed them nothing
and helped anyway.
   “It’s turning with us,” Amaia’s son said against her ribs, in a voice
too old for him. “Turn with it.”
   She did not answer. She turned the bow toward the first dark shape
of land and did not look back, and the river lost them, and the sea took
them in.
   On the bank, the man with the book finished his line and closed it. It
was a good book. It would be kept.

